/* Watch tab data. Summaries are paraphrased; videos play from YouTube (not re-hosted). Built by kb/build_watch.py */
window.WATCH={"hughes":[{"id":"GPWZ4aAtt3c","title":"Elizabeth Holmes’ interviews","who":"Elizabeth Holmes, Theranos founder (later convicted of fraud), in TV and conference interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Nov 2021","dur":"1:50:20","sum":"The panel works through several Holmes interviews. They point to a voice that seems put on, near-constant raised brows, and her switching between ‘I’ and ‘we’ when credit or blame comes up. They treat these as signs of pressure and image management, not proof by themselves.","concepts":["Deception cues","Needs & approval","Pronouns & words","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"5:50","label":"‘I’ vs ‘we’","note":"Pronouns shift with whether she is taking credit or sharing responsibility.","cue":"","ask":"pronoun-world"},{"t":"36:12","label":"Lips tighten under a money question","note":"Lip tightening and a chin movement appear right as company value is questioned.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"1:00:48","label":"Mouth snaps shut after statements","note":"Hughes flags an immediate mouth closure as soon as an answer ends.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"1:14:58","label":"Raised brows, constant nodding","note":"Brows stay up and she nods throughout: read as wanting approval.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"1:30:10","label":"Request for approval","note":"She checks how she is being received, sits more rigidly and stops using her hands.","cue":"","ask":"need-approval"}]},{"id":"LJfRU7FCmW4","title":"Chris Watts’ TV interviews","who":"Chris Watts, who later pleaded guilty to murdering his family (Colorado, 2018), in the interviews he gave before his arrest","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Feb 2021","dur":"1:32:02","sum":"The panel reads Watts’ porch interviews from before his arrest. Hughes scores several answers on his deception scale, counting cues such as a hidden inhale, lip licks, one-shoulder shrugs and the loss of pronouns. They show how many cues pile up in a few seconds, and they compare each one with his own baseline.","concepts":["Deception cues","Clusters","Baseline","Pronouns & words","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"16:51","label":"Cluster before an answer","note":"A hidden inhale, a lip lick, a one-shoulder shrug and a head shake come before he answers. Hughes scores it on his scale.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"drs-scale"},{"t":"17:43","label":"Pronouns disappear","note":"Describing the house, he suddenly stops using pronouns. Hughes reads that as distancing.","cue":"Drops 'I' from the story","ask":"pronoun-world"},{"t":"26:52","label":"Brow raise for approval","note":"As he says he called her several times, his brows lift and he glances to see if he is believed.","cue":"Glance to a third person","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"48:01","label":"Swallow, lip retraction, eye blocking","note":"Several stress cues land within the first three seconds of an answer.","cue":"Throat rises / hard swallow","ask":"cue-throat-rises-hard-swallow"},{"t":"1:28:12","label":"Using his baseline","note":"The panel sums up how the cues changed from his normal behaviour across the clips.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"uGbU84dLVNU","title":"Chris Watts: police body-cam footage","who":"Chris Watts, filmed on police body cameras on the day his family was reported missing (2018)","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Mar 2021","dur":"1:36:50","sum":"The body-cam footage shows Watts talking with officers and neighbours. The panel notes repeated eyebrow flashes, an open mouth and fast breathing, which they read as rising fight-or-flight. Hughes counts the cues on his scale and shows how fast a blink rate can jump when a key question lands.","concepts":["Deception cues","Stress cues","Clusters","Baseline","Needs & approval"],"moments":[{"t":"6:24","label":"Eyebrow flash for approval","note":"He keeps flashing his brows as if asking to be believed.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"17:30","label":"Mouth open for air","note":"An open mouth is read as the body pulling in more oxygen as stress rises.","cue":"Nostril flare","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"22:05","label":"Fast breathing","note":"They estimate a very high breathing rate at this point.","cue":"Belly → chest breathing","ask":"cue-belly-chest-breathing"},{"t":"59:02","label":"Scoring the cluster","note":"Hughes counts rocking, hidden hands, eyebrow flashes, a raised blink rate and an open mouth on his scale.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"drs-scale"},{"t":"1:18:33","label":"Blink-rate jump","note":"His blink rate jumps sharply as soon as the final question is asked.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"}]},{"id":"5t1jOZTe7iU","title":"Jodi Arias interrogation","who":"Jodi Arias, later convicted of murder (Arizona, 2013), in her police interrogation","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Feb 2021","dur":"1:48:18","sum":"The panel looks at the language and the face in Arias’ interrogation. Qualifiers tacked onto her answers, raised brows and lips that close fast after she speaks get the most attention, along with downward glances read as rehearsing what to say next.","concepts":["Deception cues","Pronouns & words","Needs & approval","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"9:12","label":"Qualifier tacked on","note":"An extra phrase added to an answer to make it sound more convincing.","cue":"'As far as I know', 'basically'","ask":"verbal-deception"},{"t":"29:10","label":"Qualifiers everywhere","note":"Almost every answer comes with a qualifier.","cue":"'As far as I know', 'basically'","ask":"verbal-deception"},{"t":"48:17","label":"Chin up, lips back","note":"Her chin lifts and her lips draw back, read as a request for approval.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"1:11:29","label":"Lips close fast","note":"Her lips close quickly right after she finishes speaking.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"1:37:10","label":"Eyes down, buying time","note":"A downward glance is read as her preparing the next line.","cue":"Unusual pause before answering","ask":"deception-is-stress"}]},{"id":"KofNh6YSFF8","title":"Scott Peterson’s TV interviews","who":"Scott Peterson, convicted in 2004 of murdering his wife Laci (his appeals continue), in TV interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Feb 2021","dur":"1:19:07","sum":"The panel breaks down Peterson’s TV interviews. They pick out lip compression, smiles that don’t fit the topic, one-shoulder shrugs and the lack of pronouns. They also warn that a smile can be part of someone’s baseline.","concepts":["Deception cues","Baseline","Pronouns & words","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"12:30","label":"Smile under pressure","note":"They caution that some people smile under stress as part of their normal baseline.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"13:05","label":"Lip compression","note":"His lips squeeze together, read as holding something back.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"14:34","label":"Smile grows on a hard word","note":"When a heavy word comes up, his smile widens. They read that as nervous energy being released.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"21:04","label":"Textbook one-shoulder shrug","note":"A one-shoulder shrug comes during a key statement, read as low confidence in what he is saying.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"1:09:11","label":"No pronouns","note":"A stretch of talk with no pronouns at all, which Hughes treats as a strong verbal flag.","cue":"Drops 'I' from the story","ask":"pronoun-world"}]},{"id":"PFYTBMfVqr0","title":"Prince Andrew’s BBC Newsnight interview","who":"Prince Andrew, in his 2019 BBC interview about his links to Jeffrey Epstein (he denies wrongdoing)","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Jun 2020","dur":"1:14:09","sum":"This is a cue-by-cue read of the 2019 BBC interview. The panel shows one-sided contempt, changes in breathing you can see from the shoulders, and a shrug plus brow flash and head tilt that they read as approval-seeking. Overall they describe the delivery as well rehearsed.","concepts":["Stress cues","Needs & approval","Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"8:18","label":"One-sided (contempt) expression","note":"Contempt is the one expression that shows on only one side of the face.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"10:59","label":"Breathing rises","note":"You can see his breathing speed up from the movement at his shoulder.","cue":"Belly → chest breathing","ask":"cue-belly-chest-breathing"},{"t":"18:47","label":"Shrug, then a brow flash","note":"A shrug as he moves into his narrative, then a brow flash and head tilt asking for approval.","cue":"Head tilt","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"1:09:57","label":"Rehearsed delivery","note":"The panel compares this with his baseline and calls the delivery almost perfectly executed.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"QQRaEm321gs","title":"Belle Gibson’s 60 Minutes interview","who":"Belle Gibson, who falsely claimed to have cured her cancer (an Australian court later fined her), in her 2015 60 Minutes interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Jan 2022","dur":"1:15:24","sum":"The panel looks at Gibson’s 60 Minutes interview. They point to small expressions of disgust, breathing you can follow at her collar and a mouth that closes the moment an answer ends. Hughes scores a few seconds of one answer on his behaviour table before she even speaks.","concepts":["Deception cues","Clusters","Baseline","Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"3:15","label":"A flicker of disgust","note":"A small disgust movement at the mouth.","cue":"Disgust","ask":"cue-disgust"},{"t":"4:18","label":"Breathing at the collar","note":"Watch her polo neck rise and fall to track her breathing rate.","cue":"Belly → chest breathing","ask":"cue-belly-chest-breathing"},{"t":"7:16","label":"Deviation from her baseline","note":"An immediate mouth closure after a statement stood out from how she normally behaves.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"57:15","label":"Vague words and qualifiers","note":"Vague language, qualifiers and hesitation in a short answer.","cue":"'As far as I know', 'basically'","ask":"verbal-deception"},{"t":"1:01:45","label":"Scored before she speaks","note":"Breathing shifts to the chest and her blink rate rises. Hughes scores this on his table before she answers.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"drs-scale"}]},{"id":"Vzoy54gMitc","title":"John Wayne Gacy: FBI interview tapes","who":"John Wayne Gacy, convicted serial killer (executed in 1994), in recorded interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Apr 2022","dur":"1:27:36","sum":"The panel sets up Gacy’s eye-movement baseline and then watches for changes. They note head tilts, lip retraction and chin movement (shame or grief), contempt expressions, distancing language and a switch to ‘we’ that softens his own role.","concepts":["Baseline","Deception cues","Pronouns & words","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"4:34","label":"Setting his baseline","note":"Where his eyes go during normal recall becomes the reference point.","cue":"Looks to one side for good memories","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"15:11","label":"Unusual head tilt","note":"A head tilt that isn’t part of his baseline, at a key statement.","cue":"Head tilt","ask":"cue-head-tilt"},{"t":"15:52","label":"Lip retraction, chin movement","note":"Lip retraction and chin movement, read as shame or grief, alongside distancing words.","cue":"Lips pulled in / bitten","ask":"cue-lips-pulled-in-bitten"},{"t":"40:58","label":"Contempt, then a shrug","note":"A contempt expression, then a one-shoulder shrug as he dismisses it.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"48:53","label":"Switch to ‘we’","note":"Team pronouns soften his own part in the story.","cue":"Drops 'I' from the story","ask":"pronoun-world"}]},{"id":"xkm0vx0xiaI","title":"Richard Kuklinski (‘the Iceman’) interview","who":"Richard Kuklinski, convicted contract killer, in a prison documentary interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Dec 2021","dur":"1:08:34","sum":"The panel warns that a baseline can itself be a deceptive one, so they keep asking questions. They point to small contempt flashes and a rare moment where chin trembling betrays emotion he is trying to swallow.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"3:19","label":"A baseline for deception?","note":"They warn that the baseline you collect might be someone’s deceptive baseline, so you keep asking.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"36:49","label":"Mild contempt","note":"His lip rises slightly at the end of a story.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"51:30","label":"Chin trembles","note":"Chin and lower-mouth trembling as he chokes back emotion.","cue":"","ask":""}]},{"id":"moKoQPAyrWs","title":"Richard Ramirez (‘Night Stalker’) interview","who":"Richard Ramirez, convicted serial killer, in a jailhouse interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Jan 2021","dur":"1:16:11","sum":"Hughes picks out genuine smiles, which come when Ramirez is compared with other notorious killers. The panel reads that, together with his chin rising, as pride and a need for significance. They also cover a fixed, low-blink stare and lines that sound rehearsed but unpractised.","concepts":["Needs & approval","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"14:12","label":"Genuine smile at recognition","note":"He gives a real smile when he is ranked alongside other killers.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"need-significance"},{"t":"18:07","label":"Fixed, low-blink stare","note":"A fixed stare with very little blinking.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"23:04","label":"Chin rises with pride","note":"His chin lifts when he is compared with others: significance.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"need-significance"},{"t":"49:14","label":"Brow raise, then disdain","note":"A brow raise looking for approval, followed by disdain.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"}]},{"id":"XZuvFL_u0yg","title":"Anna Delvey (Sorokin) interviews","who":"Anna Sorokin (‘Anna Delvey’), convicted of fraud in New York in 2019, in interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Mar 2022","dur":"2:04:13","sum":"The panel looks at whether Sorokin’s confidence is real. They build her eye-accessing baseline, then flag pride displays (chin up, head back), brow raises asking for approval and small contempt expressions. They also explain why baseline matters so much.","concepts":["Baseline","Needs & approval","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"14:15","label":"Eye-accessing baseline","note":"Where her eyes go during normal recall becomes the reference point.","cue":"Looks to one side for good memories","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"29:25","label":"Pride display","note":"Her chin goes up and her head goes back with a smile, read as pride.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"need-significance"},{"t":"30:02","label":"Brow raise for approval","note":"A repeating pattern of brow raises as she seeks approval.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"55:41","label":"Expressions that fade","note":"When she is more honest, her mouth stays open and expressions fade slowly instead of vanishing.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"1:14:04","label":"Micro-contempt","note":"A tiny contempt expression, possibly aimed at her old self.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"}]},{"id":"1VAkvSFDsuk","title":"Robinhood CEO under questioning","who":"Vlad Tenev, Robinhood CEO, in a 2021 interview about the GameStop trading halt (not accused of a crime)","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Feb 2021","dur":"1:07:29","sum":"This is a good example of pressure without any crime. The panel reads Tenev’s constant brow raises and repeated shrugs as apologetic and approval-seeking, and points to a big swallow when the interviewer challenges him. They also show how to watch breathing at the throat notch.","concepts":["Stress cues","Needs & approval","Baseline"],"moments":[{"t":"4:08","label":"Shrug, head shake, eye block","note":"Three cues together as he denies a claim.","cue":"Both shoulders shrug","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"4:56","label":"Approval-seeking tone","note":"Constant brow raises and an apologetic tone throughout.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"11:56","label":"Big swallow","note":"A visible swallow when he is told he is doing the opposite of what the business promises.","cue":"Throat rises / hard swallow","ask":"cue-throat-rises-hard-swallow"},{"t":"20:23","label":"Watch the throat notch","note":"How to read breathing rate from the notch at the base of the throat.","cue":"Belly → chest breathing","ask":"cue-belly-chest-breathing"},{"t":"42:23","label":"Shrug plus a confirmation glance","note":"A one-shoulder shrug, then a glance at the host to check how it landed.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"}]},{"id":"fdXv-AdCksc","title":"Biden vs Trump: first 2020 debate","who":"Joe Biden and Donald Trump at the first 2020 presidential debate","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Oct 2020","dur":"1:04:05","sum":"This is a debate breakdown rather than a lie hunt. The panel watches who backs away to make space, mouth movements and blink rate rising under attack, rehearsed lines versus lines delivered under stress, and hands that stop matching the speech rhythm once a speaker is interrupted.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"26:47","label":"Creating distance","note":"Backing away to create space is read as a stress response.","cue":"Leans away","ask":"proxemics"},{"t":"30:53","label":"Smile drops, blinks rise","note":"A broad smile turns into pursed lips, and blinks rise as soon as he is attacked.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"42:35","label":"Flashes of contempt","note":"Brief contempt expressions during exchanges.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"43:59","label":"Hands lose the beat","note":"On message, his hands move in time with his words. The more he is interrupted, the less they match.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"Rcy7n_HOguk","title":"Will Smith and Chris Rock at the Oscars","who":"Will Smith and Chris Rock at the 2022 Oscars","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Apr 2022","dur":"20:08","sum":"This short episode looks at what came before and after the slap. Rock’s hands go behind his back as he takes it, and Smith shifts his balance onto his dominant foot. The panel’s safety tip is to watch the shoulders, because people have to move them before any physical action.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"8:10","label":"Hands behind the back","note":"Rock takes it the way a soldier takes a dressing-down, hands behind his back.","cue":"Hands behind back","ask":"cue-hands-behind-back"},{"t":"12:31","label":"Weight onto the dominant foot","note":"Smith shifts his balance as if ready to fight.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"18:31","label":"Watch shoulders before violence","note":"Anyone about to move has to move their shoulders first.","cue":"Shoulders stay raised","ask":""}]},{"id":"iksaUTFRhT4","title":"What ‘on message’ looks like: Biden at the 2020 debate","who":"Joe Biden at the 2020 presidential debates","src":"Chase Hughes on Newsmax (his YouTube channel)","by":"Chase Hughes, interviewed by Grant Stinchfield","date":"Oct 2020","dur":"3:09","sum":"A three-minute TV clip in which Hughes explains what he looks for. Rehearsed, on-message lines come with deliberate eye contact with the camera and even, symmetrical gestures that keep time with the speech. He stresses that no single behaviour means deception or honesty: you are reading comfort and stress.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"0:07","label":"On-message behaviour","note":"Deliberate eye contact and symmetrical gestures that keep time with the words.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"0:47","label":"No single ‘lie’ behaviour","note":"Hughes says there is no one behaviour for honesty or deception: look for comfort or stress.","cue":"","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"1:45","label":"Turning to the camera","note":"Looking straight into the camera is read as a scripted, rehearsed line.","cue":"","ask":""}]},{"id":"6VLZWC90eXQ","title":"R. Kelly’s interview with Gayle King","who":"R. Kelly, convicted of racketeering and sex trafficking (2021) and child sex crimes (2022), in his 2019 CBS interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Apr 2021","dur":"1:32:02","sum":"The panel reads Kelly’s eyebrow flashes as requests for approval, which they say celebrities use a lot. They track where he places past and future on his timeline. Around his denials they see a large cluster: head shakes, eye blocks, lip suppression, a one-shoulder shrug and turtling. They also remind viewers that clusters, not single cues, carry weight.","concepts":["Clusters","Needs & approval","Deception cues"],"moments":[{"t":"10:15","label":"Brow flash for approval","note":"Celebrities often use brow flashes to win approval.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"32:05","label":"You need a cluster","note":"A reminder that it takes a cluster to call truth or lie.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"46:29","label":"A big cluster","note":"Head shakes, eye blocks, lip suppression, self-soothing, a one-shoulder shrug and turtling, all together.","cue":"Turtling (shoulders up, head down)","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"35:41","label":"Chin-up indignation","note":"He lifts his chin in a show of indignation.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":""}]},{"id":"-M-_u45wgTc","title":"Andrew Cuomo’s apology statement","who":"Andrew Cuomo, then New York governor, in his 2021 video statement after a report on harassment allegations (he denied wrongdoing and resigned)","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Aug 2021","dur":"1:51:44","sum":"The panel uses Cuomo’s statement to show baseline work. A tongue-jut is just his normal preparation to speak, not a tell. Against that, they count his blink rate, note a sideways smirk at the word ‘inappropriately’ and a mouth that snaps shut, and read his shoulders punctuating the lines as reading from a screen.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues","Emotion","Needs & approval"],"moments":[{"t":"8:53","label":"Sideways smirk","note":"A one-sided smirk as he says the word ‘inappropriately’.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"21:03","label":"Counting blinks","note":"Hughes counts a high blink rate over less than a minute.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"22:18","label":"Part of his baseline","note":"A tongue movement that is just his normal preparation to speak. Not a tell.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:05:29","label":"Mouth snaps shut","note":"An immediate mouth closure that differs from his other answers.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"1:09:13","label":"Brows up","note":"Brows go up: a request for approval, or emphasis.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"}]},{"id":"ldIVCDFlFms","title":"Erin Patterson’s police interview","who":"Erin Patterson, convicted in 2025 of the mushroom-lunch murders (Victoria, Australia), in her police interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Sep 2025","dur":"29:24","sum":"Hughes uses a cabinet in the background as a reference point to show how her eyes move away right after a relevant question. Then he explains what a cluster is: eye blocking, very restrained nodding and strong lip compression. The panel also notes she answers the question she thinks is being asked, not the one that was put to her.","concepts":["Clusters","Baseline","Deception cues","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"16:19","label":"A visual reference point","note":"He uses a background object to track where her eyes go after each question.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"16:33","label":"What a cluster is","note":"Several behaviours together, not one on its own.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"17:08","label":"Hard lip compression","note":"Lips squeezed tightly together.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"23:57","label":"Answers the implied question","note":"She answers what she thinks she is being accused of, not the question asked.","cue":"Answer doesn't answer","ask":"verbal-deception"}]},{"id":"xERieqYufnU","title":"YouTuber apology videos","who":"Public apology videos by YouTubers PewDiePie, Laura Lee, Logan Paul and Tana Mongeau","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Nov 2020","dur":"56:04","sum":"Here the panel reads apology videos for sincerity rather than crimes. They point to lip puckering after ‘I’m ashamed’ (disagreeing with your own words), eyelids that close and open fast, rehearsed delivery with no hand movement, a freeze response, and tears allowed to roll on camera.","concepts":["Emotion","Stress cues","Needs & approval"],"moments":[{"t":"7:24","label":"Sour-taste lips","note":"Lip puckering right after ‘I’m ashamed’, read as disagreeing with his own statement.","cue":"Lips pulled in / bitten","ask":"cue-lips-pulled-in-bitten"},{"t":"9:29","label":"Eyelid ‘shutter’","note":"His eyes close and open fast, the fear cue Hughes calls shutter speed.","cue":"Fast eyelid 'shutter'","ask":"cue-fast-eyelid-shutter"},{"t":"10:29","label":"No hand movement","note":"A rehearsed delivery with no hand movement.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"13:19","label":"Freeze","note":"He goes still: the freeze part of fear.","cue":"Freeze","ask":"cue-freeze"},{"t":"25:11","label":"Eyes asking for approval","note":"Eye movements read as a request for approval.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"}]},{"id":"D3YSKYAz23k","title":"Aileen Wuornos interview","who":"Aileen Wuornos, convicted serial killer (executed in 2002), in a filmed interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Dec 2021","dur":"1:31:29","sum":"The panel uses Wuornos’ very low blink rate as a sign of intense focus. They set her baseline for truthful, proud recall, then flag a one-shoulder shrug, lip licking right before a key phrase and chin movement (shame or grief). Their verdict on her apology is that it never actually owns the acts.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Needs & approval","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"3:48","label":"Blink rate near zero","note":"Very few blinks: intense focus.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"14:42","label":"Baseline for truthful recall","note":"Where her eyes go when she recalls something proudly and truthfully.","cue":"Looks to one side for good memories","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"15:30","label":"One-shoulder shrug","note":"She isn’t confident in what she is saying.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"1:02:08","label":"Lip compression and chin movement","note":"A first hint of shame or guilt.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"1:19:20","label":"Apology without ownership","note":"She apologises that events happened, never for what she did.","cue":"Softer word for the act ('took' not 'stole')","ask":"verbal-deception"}]},{"id":"EdCg9LAy3sY","title":"The Menendez brothers’ interviews","who":"Lyle and Erik Menendez, convicted in 1996 of killing their parents (resentenced in 2025), in interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Apr 2021","dur":"1:36:21","sum":"This episode is careful and balanced. The panel compares how long each eyebrow flash lasts, spots a flash of anger at the word ‘abuse’, and shows how rehearsed memories differ from emotional recall. They also mark stretches where his recall matches his message as good baseline.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"11:53","label":"Comparing brow flashes","note":"This flash is shorter than his others.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"cue-eyebrow-flash"},{"t":"20:21","label":"Anger at a word","note":"His brows pull down at the word ‘abuse’ in the question.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"52:52","label":"Emotional recall","note":"Hand movements and a brow flash during emotional recall.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:05:41","label":"Congruent: good baseline","note":"His recall matches the message, so it serves as good baseline.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:28:50","label":"‘We’ for bad news","note":"More team pronouns when the information is negative.","cue":"Drops 'I' from the story","ask":"pronoun-world"}]},{"id":"IwCx5BhfvmQ","title":"Richard Allen’s second interrogation (Delphi)","who":"Richard Allen, convicted in 2024 of the Delphi murders (Indiana), in his police interrogation","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"1:33:42","sum":"The panel watches Allen's second interrogation. They start from his baseline and look for deviations: a foot flex when test results come up, and controlled thumb adaptors. Anger that looks overacted rather than real, contempt as he nods along to the evidence, and arms pinned 'like a T-Rex' are read as fear of making a scene. Late on he locks into a figure-four guard with a jittering foot. They also critique the interrogators' technique.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues","Deception cues","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"4:27","label":"Baseline first","note":"Look for this person's normal, then for deviations.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"34:07","label":"Anger that's overacted","note":"Rage that doesn't look like real anger.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"},{"t":"37:35","label":"Nodding with contempt","note":"He nods along while contempt shows.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"53:24","label":"T-Rex arms","note":"Gestures pinned close, afraid to make a scene.","cue":"Elbows pull in","ask":"cue-elbows-pull-in"},{"t":"1:12:31","label":"Figure-four guard","note":"A defensive leg lock with a jittering foot.","cue":"Locked ankles / legs bound tight","ask":"cue-locked-ankles-legs-bound-tight"}]},{"id":"uU3XA9Rvpik","title":"Denali Brehmer’s interviews (‘catfish’ murder)","who":"Denali Brehmer, who pleaded guilty in 2023 to murdering her friend Cynthia Hoffman (Alaska), in police and prison interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"1:46:56","sum":"The panel reads Brehmer's interviews. Her voice lifts in uncertainty, and an eyelid squeeze and downward glances come as she searches for answers. A neck scratch and moving legs show her nervous system catching up, and her jaw grinds when the plan is pinned on her. She qualifies her answers with 'I'm not trying to fully…'. When she later talks about having changed, they see no emotional leakage. They describe her as detached rather than labelling her.","concepts":["Stress cues","Pronouns & words","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"6:10","label":"Uncertain upward inflection","note":"Her voice lifts on 'believed'.","cue":"Pitch rises slightly","ask":"cue-pitch-rises-slightly"},{"t":"32:08","label":"Neck scratch, legs move","note":"Her nervous system 'finally throwing' signals.","cue":"Back of neck rub","ask":"cue-back-of-neck-rub"},{"t":"1:03:04","label":"Qualified answers","note":"'I'm not trying to fully…' softens the claim.","cue":"'As far as I know', 'basically'","ask":"cue-as-far-as-i-know-basically"},{"t":"1:04:54","label":"Jaw grinding","note":"The first time, when she has nowhere to go.","cue":"Jaw clench","ask":"cue-jaw-clench"},{"t":"1:36:32","label":"No emotional leakage","note":"No tremor or shame during talk of change.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"}]},{"id":"iB4SdBBqYo4","title":"Virginia McCullough’s police interviews","who":"Virginia McCullough, who pleaded guilty to murdering her parents (Essex, England) and was sentenced in 2024, in police interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"1:17:35","sum":"The panel contrasts McCullough's calm, helpful manner with what's missing. They note a gap between her public and private voices, blink-rate spikes while she searches for answers, constant eyebrow flashes with parted lips, and a fast one-sided contempt. Lips are withdrawn and the chin tucks with eye avoidance at the point where shame shows. Only late, after a money admission, does sadness briefly group the brow.","concepts":["Emotion","Needs & approval","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"11:16","label":"Public vs private voice","note":"Two distinct voices for two audiences.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"13:36","label":"Blink spike while searching","note":"Linked to searching memory, not to shock.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"16:33","label":"Forehead up for approval","note":"Raised brows with large illustrators.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"26:14","label":"Fast contempt","note":"Narrowed eyes and a one-sided mouth.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"29:20","label":"Shame: chin tuck, eyes away","note":"Lips forward, chin tucked, eye avoidance.","cue":"Turtling (shoulders up, head down)","ask":"cue-turtling-shoulders-up-head-down"}]},{"id":"g0B-H1VrF9U","title":"Megan Boswell’s police interviews","who":"Megan Boswell, convicted in 2024 of murdering her daughter Evelyn (Tennessee), in police and media interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"2:04:16","sum":"The panel's second look at Boswell centres on approval-seeking. Her forehead stays raised in a 'request for approval', her up-talk softens every claim, and a pleasant smile seems out of place for a mother of a missing child. Her blink rate is low while she watches the officers. Her hands move from comfort to under her legs as stress builds. They note stress, but not the stress of worry for a child.","concepts":["Needs & approval","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"11:59","label":"Request for approval","note":"Forehead up while making a claim.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"16:24","label":"A pleasant smile","note":"Out of keeping with the situation.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"},{"t":"18:26","label":"Low blink rate","note":"She keeps watching the officers.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"28:39","label":"Hands under the legs","note":"An adaptor as stress builds.","cue":"Hands clasp knees","ask":"cue-hands-clasp-knees"},{"t":"1:02:40","label":"The wrong kind of stress","note":"Stress, but no grief or worry.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"}]},{"id":"2okP3y8D9jg","title":"Ryan Routh’s 2022 interviews","who":"Ryan Routh, convicted in 2025 of attempting to assassinate Donald Trump (Florida, 2024), in 2022 media interviews about volunteering in Ukraine","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"1:20:26","sum":"This episode is mostly about language and mindset rather than body language. The panel notes black-and-white, good-versus-evil framing and repeated virtue words that cast himself as morally superior. They also note his insistent demands and emotion tied to symbolic heroism, and they describe a 'true believer' profile. They caution against reading a crooked smile as contempt when it may simply be how a face is built.","concepts":["Pronouns & words","Emotion","Baseline"],"moments":[{"t":"14:34","label":"Black-and-white framing","note":"Conflict framed as pure good versus evil.","cue":"","ask":"yt-identity-trap"},{"t":"15:21","label":"Virtue words on repeat","note":"Repeated virtue language implies moral superiority.","cue":"","ask":"identity-belief"},{"t":"40:16","label":"A crooked smile isn't contempt","note":"Some faces are just asymmetric, so check the baseline.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"52:14","label":"Emotion for symbols","note":"Feeling attached to heroism and symbols, not to harm.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"55:37","label":"The true believer","note":"Leaning in with conviction, a fixed mindset.","cue":"Leans toward you","ask":"yt-identity-trap"}]},{"id":"BDuNC6Z0wjg","title":"Brian Cohee’s police interviews","who":"Brian Cohee, convicted of the 2019 murder of Kenneth Mayne (California), in police interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"2:02:08","sum":"The panel treats this as a case of what a lack of emotion looks like in custody. They note a confirmation glance as he gauges his likely sentence, hard eye contact with arms crossed when he talks about trust, and a blink rate that doesn't move while he describes the killing. He leans back to lecture detectives, then leans away when he might offend. Under pressure he digs into his nail beds.","concepts":["Emotion","Stress cues","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"3:34","label":"Confirmation glance","note":"Checking a reaction while gauging his sentence.","cue":"Glance to a third person","ask":"cue-glance-to-a-third-person"},{"t":"13:24","label":"Hard eye contact, arms cross","note":"The start of a repeated pattern.","cue":"Arms crossed (relaxed fingers)","ask":"cue-arms-crossed-relaxed-fingers"},{"t":"33:57","label":"Blink rate stays flat","note":"No change while describing violence.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"1:08:21","label":"Leans away","note":"When he senses he may offend the detective.","cue":"Leans away","ask":"cue-leans-away"},{"t":"1:28:29","label":"Digging into nail beds","note":"Adapting through self-inflicted pain.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"}]},{"id":"vWv9ScFo_NU","title":"Robert Telles on the stand","who":"Robert Telles, convicted in 2024 of murdering journalist Jeff German (Las Vegas), testifying at his trial","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"1:55:15","sum":"The panel counts Telles' tells on the stand. Eyebrow flashes seek social approval, and a short, apprehensive head nod comes with faster breathing. A contempt micro-expression appears when he is called amateurish. His blink rate spikes on hard questions, and a head tilt shows deference. They also run a checklist for rehearsed testimony: forced expressions, script-like speech and long pauses.","concepts":["Stress cues","Needs & approval","Deception cues"],"moments":[{"t":"4:39","label":"Eyebrow flash for approval","note":"Mixed with offered confusion.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"11:56","label":"Short, apprehensive nod","note":"With a rise in breathing.","cue":"","ask":"nodding"},{"t":"13:38","label":"Contempt micro-expression","note":"When the prosecutor calls him amateurish.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"20:19","label":"Head tilt","note":"Deference: exposing the neck.","cue":"Head tilt","ask":"cue-head-tilt"},{"t":"40:12","label":"Signs of rehearsed testimony","note":"Pausing as if recalling lines.","cue":"Unusual pause before answering","ask":"cue-unusual-pause-before-answering"}]},{"id":"fhgXFEbdHzs","title":"Susan Smith’s parole hearing","who":"Susan Smith, convicted in 1995 of murdering her two sons (South Carolina), at her 2024 parole hearing","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"36:56","sum":"The panel revisits Smith at her parole hearing. When she talks about shame, the chin boss doesn't move. Her tone, cadence and gestures don't line up with her words, which they call 'gesture arrhythmia', and she eye-blocks without looking at the board. There's a lot of lip licking, retraction and compression that wasn't in her earlier baseline, plus a qualifier ('really') inside a denial. Her nostrils flare as stress catches up with her.","concepts":["Emotion","Stress cues","Baseline","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"0:47","label":"No chin-boss movement","note":"The shame and grief marker is missing.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"1:51","label":"Gesture arrhythmia","note":"Illustrators out of step with her words.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"8:42","label":"Lip licking and retraction","note":"Not part of her earlier baseline.","cue":"Lips pulled in / bitten","ask":"cue-lips-pulled-in-bitten"},{"t":"15:08","label":"A qualifier in a denial","note":"'Really' in the middle of a denial.","cue":"'As far as I know', 'basically'","ask":"cue-as-far-as-i-know-basically"},{"t":"34:26","label":"Hidden breathing","note":"Nostril flaring to get air without opening the mouth.","cue":"Nostril flare","ask":"cue-nostril-flare"}]},{"id":"U6u8dlYXcS4","title":"DA Sandra Doorley’s traffic stop and apology","who":"Sandra Doorley, Monroe County (New York) district attorney, in police bodycam from a 2025 traffic stop and her video apology (she was not charged with a crime)","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"1:59:53","sum":"The panel contrasts the bodycam with the later apology. During the stop they see no shaking hands or worry, but targeting eyes and a beating brow that read as anger. In the apology they see a chin jut at 'an officer doing their job', disgust at 'I failed you' and a one-shoulder shrug on 'all officers deserve respect'. Lip compression comes with the excuse, and her tone rises in a request for approval.","concepts":["Emotion","Needs & approval","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"4:50","label":"No sign of worry","note":"No shaking hands during the stop.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"22:29","label":"Targeting eyes","note":"Anger shown with the eyes and a beating brow.","cue":"Squint","ask":"cue-squint"},{"t":"1:01:18","label":"Chin jut","note":"Disagreement on 'an officer doing their job'.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"cue-chin-thrust"},{"t":"1:01:42","label":"A shrug on 'respect'","note":"Suggests a lack of conviction.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"1:30:09","label":"Request for approval","note":"Her voice goes up where it should come down.","cue":"","ask":"need-approval"}]},{"id":"nEIjlhlCT5M","title":"Trump vs Harris: what to watch for","who":"Donald Trump and Kamala Harris, in clips the panel reviewed ahead of their September 2024 debate","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"1:02:58","sum":"Before the debate, the panel builds a baseline for each candidate so viewers can spot changes on the night. They cover each one's habitual word swaps and hand movements under pressure. They describe what confident delivery looks like for each: brows, illustrators on the beat, confirmation nods in the right places, and steady cadence. Both become more fluid when confident and less so when they're not.","concepts":["Baseline","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"11:46","label":"Word choice is baseline","note":"Habitual word swaps aren't red flags.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"20:10","label":"A pressure gesture","note":"A habitual hand move that appears more under pressure.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"39:49","label":"Nods where they belong","note":"Illustrators and nods on point when she believes it.","cue":"","ask":"nodding"},{"t":"40:06","label":"Steady cadence and tone","note":"Volume and tone that don't waver.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"},{"t":"59:58","label":"Fluid when confident","note":"Body and face flow; watch for the moment they stiffen.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"ySCWGOL0Q0M","title":"Gypsy Rose Blanchard’s interviews","who":"Gypsy Rose Blanchard, who pleaded guilty to second-degree murder over her mother's 2015 death (Missouri) and was released in 2023, in interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"1:45:32","sum":"The panel mostly reads Blanchard as honest, which shows how deviations stand out against a baseline. She illustrates with her whole hands and body, speaks comfortably and keeps good eye contact with a low blink rate. Then a big change: eye closure while she speaks and lip-licking with a tongue jut on the hardest topics. A pronounced shoulder shrug comes on 'I wish it wouldn't have happened that way'.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"15:26","label":"Whole-body illustrators","note":"Comfortable, open delivery.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"},{"t":"23:50","label":"Eye contact, low blinks","note":"Part of her baseline here.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"48:54","label":"A giant baseline deviation","note":"Eye-blocking while she talks is new.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"50:09","label":"Lip-lick with a tongue jut","note":"An early 'no' signal on a hard topic.","cue":"Lips pulled in / bitten","ask":"cue-lips-pulled-in-bitten"},{"t":"1:07:02","label":"The biggest shrug","note":"On 'I wish it wouldn't have happened that way'.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"}]},{"id":"0Rcqjp4osXM","title":"Diane Downs’ interviews","who":"Diane Downs, convicted in 1984 of shooting her three children, one fatally (Oregon), in TV interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"37:29","sum":"The panel focuses on shallow affect. Downs describes her children being shot in a flat, mechanical voice, like a narrator reading a script, with no rise in pitch where pain would push it up. She holds romancer eye contact to check the effect and shrugs one shoulder on 'I guess'. When challenged she sets her jaw, purses her lips and switches to a hard tone, with an eye flutter on 'insane' and 'crazy'.","concepts":["Emotion","Stress cues","Deception cues"],"moments":[{"t":"2:02","label":"Shallow affect","note":"Reduced emotion where a lot is expected.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"5:00","label":"Mechanical delivery","note":"Pitch, tone and cadence don't carry the pain.","cue":"Pitch rises slightly","ask":"cue-pitch-rises-slightly"},{"t":"5:39","label":"One shoulder up on 'I guess'","note":"Uncertainty in her own account.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"10:31","label":"Challenged: jaw set, lips pursed","note":"Hard eye contact and a tone change.","cue":"Jaw clench","ask":"cue-jaw-clench"},{"t":"11:57","label":"Eye flutter","note":"Rapid blinking on 'insane' and 'crazy'.","cue":"Fast eyelid 'shutter'","ask":"cue-fast-eyelid-shutter"}]},{"id":"XEi1Qd0HnrI","title":"George Santos’ interviews","who":"George Santos, former US congressman, who pleaded guilty in 2024 to wire fraud and identity theft (his sentence was commuted in 2025), in TV interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"2:27:23","sum":"The panel works through Santos' interviews about his fabricated résumé. They see contempt or pride on being called a bad liar, and unusual eye-blocking with long 'shutter' blinks when he isn't believed. A one-shoulder shrug comes with a chin turn, and pitch and speed rise in fear. A sharp blink spike appears exactly where they'd expect stress, and his hands squeeze white-knuckled as hard questions land.","concepts":["Deception cues","Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"8:04","label":"Contempt at 'bad liar'","note":"Possibly pride in his abilities.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"11:55","label":"Long 'shutter' blinks","note":"His eyes stay closed too long when he isn't believed.","cue":"Fast eyelid 'shutter'","ask":"cue-fast-eyelid-shutter"},{"t":"12:13","label":"Shrug with chin turn","note":"A pairing the panel treats as significant.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"47:23","label":"A sharp blink spike","note":"At the exact moment stress would be expected.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"1:10:36","label":"White-knuckle hand squeeze","note":"Pacifying as the question comes.","cue":"Fingers curl (digital flexion)","ask":"cue-fingers-curl-digital-flexion"}]},{"id":"GYfhGLrrDts","title":"Charlie Adelson on the stand","who":"Charlie Adelson, convicted in 2023 of murder-for-hire in the killing of law professor Dan Markel (Florida), testifying at his trial","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"","dur":"1:52:31","sum":"The panel reads Adelson's testimony and the prosecutor's reactions. They explain illustrators versus regulators, and show that a prosecutor's raised brows with a half smile signal disbelief, not a request for approval. Adelson's breathing and halting cadence change under pressure. A brighter-eyed smile may be genuine, and a rehearsed answer isn't automatically deceptive. A late cluster combines a chest-breathing shift, blink increase, leaning back and lip compression. Their key point: look for deviation and clusters, not single gestures.","concepts":["Clusters","Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"3:19","label":"Illustrators vs regulators","note":"Making a point versus controlling the conversation.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"34:32","label":"Disbelief, not approval","note":"Brows up with amused eyes and a half smile.","cue":"","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"1:22:03","label":"A genuine moment","note":"A slight smile with brighter eyes.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"},{"t":"1:28:09","label":"Rehearsed isn't lying","note":"Preparation for an expected question.","cue":"","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"1:37:21","label":"A stress cluster","note":"Chest breathing, blinks, a lean back and lip compression.","cue":"Belly → chest breathing","ask":"clusters"}]}],"others":[{"id":"KQgObU77lV8","title":"Gangsters’ body language, 1920s–50s","who":"Mobsters such as Bugsy Siegel, in arrest and courtroom photos from the 1920s to the 1950s","src":"WIRED (Tradecraft)","by":"Joe Navarro, former FBI agent","date":"Aug 2019","dur":"4:40","sum":"Navarro looks past the tough-guy image in old mobster photos. He finds a worried brow under a sharp suit, a self-hug after an arrest, a sock-pull that cools the body, a pinch at the bridge of the nose, and a lawyer’s compressed lips that seem to say the case is lost.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"1:18","label":"Self-hug after arrest","note":"Hide the face and you see a man comforting himself.","cue":"Self-hug (palms on body)","ask":"cue-self-hug-palms-on-body"},{"t":"2:19","label":"Pulling up socks","note":"Ventilating: overheated skin gets cooled.","cue":"Ventilating (collar pull, hair off neck)","ask":"cue-ventilating-collar-pull-hair-off-neck"},{"t":"2:42","label":"Eyes closed, nose pinched","note":"A near-universal ‘I don’t like this’ behaviour.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"3:09","label":"The lawyer’s compressed lips","note":"Lips pressed together with the corners turned down.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"}]},{"id":"CfNEHW3qgso","title":"Political body language","who":"US presidential candidates, mainly Trump and Biden at the 2020 debates","src":"WIRED","by":"Joe Navarro, former FBI agent","date":"Oct 2020","dur":"12:31","sum":"Navarro explains that debates are rehearsed theatre. He covers what he watches during hard questions: lip biting, collar-pulling, throat-clearing, shoulders, thumbs and steepling as signs of confidence or doubt. Then he describes Trump’s and Biden’s typical mouth and brow habits, and stresses that disappearing lips mean stress, not lying.","concepts":["Stress cues","Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"3:20","label":"What to watch on hard questions","note":"Lip biting or a collar pull as the moderator asks.","cue":"Ventilating (collar pull, hair off neck)","ask":"cue-ventilating-collar-pull-hair-off-neck"},{"t":"4:00","label":"Hesitation and shoulders","note":"Throat-clearing and a raised shoulder show low confidence.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"4:17","label":"Thumbs and steepling","note":"Thumbs up and steepled fingers show confidence. Interlaced fingers show struggle.","cue":"Steepled fingers","ask":"cue-steepled-fingers"},{"t":"9:42","label":"A speaker’s own habits","note":"A mouth-corner pinch and lip purse as personal baseline signals.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"10:49","label":"Lips disappear: stress, not lies","note":"He is explicit that this is stress, not proof of deception.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"deception-is-stress"}]},{"id":"FtC8vIpYSmM","title":"Trump’s 2016 convention speech","who":"Donald Trump (and Ivanka Trump) at the 2016 Republican National Convention","src":"Mark Bowden (TV clip)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"Jul 2016","dur":"3:13","sum":"In this short TV clip, Bowden says a teleprompter calmed Trump’s usual gestures. He reads the ‘OK’ hand as control and detail, and the chin-out profile as a ‘hit me, I can take it’ display. He also covers Ivanka’s downward intonation for authority.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"0:48","label":"Teleprompter calms gestures","note":"Reading a script reduced his usual movement.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"1:20","label":"The ‘OK’ hand","note":"A long-running gesture signalling control and detail.","cue":"Palms open","ask":""},{"t":"1:51","label":"Chin-out profile","note":"A ‘go on, hit me’ jaw display seen in many leaders.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"cue-chin-thrust"},{"t":"2:32","label":"Downward intonation","note":"A lower, falling voice gives power at the end of points.","cue":"","ask":""}]},{"id":"ymzg8LY5xGs","title":"The royal family on camera","who":"Members of the British royal family at public events (Meghan, Prince William, Prince Andrew)","src":"The Nerve with Maureen Callahan","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"Sep 2025","dur":"14:00","sum":"Bowden reads royal appearances. He points to a smile with no eye involvement, lip compression that suggests a held-back opinion, a hair-grooming display, and Prince William keeping his body turned away while Andrew whispers to him.","concepts":["Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"4:35","label":"A smile without the eyes","note":"Only the mouth corners turn up, with no narrowing of the eyes.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"},{"t":"4:52","label":"Held-back opinion","note":"Lip compression, possibly a withheld view or anger.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"6:07","label":"Grooming display","note":"Hair-touching as a display of status.","cue":"Grooming (hair, lint, straighten up)","ask":"cue-grooming-hair-lint-straighten-up"},{"t":"9:32","label":"Body stays turned away","note":"William turns his head but not his body toward Andrew, then rubs his nose.","cue":"Torso turns away","ask":"cue-torso-turns-away"}]},{"id":"yD43e5bqjV4","title":"Prince Harry’s WellChild interview","who":"Prince Harry, in an interview at the WellChild Awards","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse","date":"Oct 2026","dur":"14:29","sum":"Rouse, a Behavior Panel member, reads Harry on his own channel. He notes a grimace with tight lips and a short breath, smiles that vanish too fast to be real, a one-shoulder shrug before Harry turns to his work, and a blink flutter as a personal habit.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion","Baseline"],"moments":[{"t":"0:57","label":"Tight lips and a short breath","note":"Read as stress before an answer that needs to carry emotion.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"1:16","label":"A smile that vanishes","note":"A smile that drops too fast to be real.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"7:17","label":"One-shoulder shrug","note":"Read as discomfort as he moves on to his work.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"12:41","label":"Blink flutter","note":"A habit of his, so it is part of his baseline.","cue":"Fast eyelid 'shutter'","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"0g411NFWmq0","title":"Prince Harry’s GQ interview","who":"Prince Harry, in a 2025 GQ interview","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"19:22","sum":"Rouse reads Harry answer by answer. He points to a deep breath at the start, feet that look ready to leave during harder questions and relax later, a falling blink rate as stress eases, a one-shoulder shrug on an uncertain answer, and smooth, illustrated delivery on answers that look prepared.","concepts":["Stress cues","Baseline","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"0:24","label":"A big breath at the start","note":"He reads it as uncertainty about the setting, not deception.","cue":"","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"0:52","label":"Feet set to leave","note":"Foot position that looks ready to get up and go.","cue":"Feet turn to the exit","ask":"cue-feet-turn-to-the-exit"},{"t":"3:51","label":"Blink rate drops","note":"Read as stress easing as the topic changes.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"12:01","label":"One-shoulder shrug","note":"Read as uncertainty about his own answer.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"15:03","label":"Smooth, illustrated answer","note":"Fluent hands and narration suggest a prepared answer.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"}]},{"id":"YxKTQV5RdwU","title":"Caleb Flynn at his sentencing (NewsNation)","who":"Caleb Flynn, convicted in September 2026 of murdering his wife Ashley (Ohio), at his sentencing","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"6:46","sum":"In a NewsNation segment, Rouse discusses Flynn’s tears and trembling at sentencing. He notes squeezed eyes and tears that are never wiped, and no visible activity in the muscles usually seen in grief. He reads the distress as self-pity rather than grief, and says the display was aimed at the judge.","concepts":["Emotion","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"2:38","label":"Squeezed eyes, tears never wiped","note":"He reads it as pushing tears out, but notes that tears alone prove nothing.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"3:17","label":"No grief markers","note":"No movement in the chin and brow muscles usually seen in grief.","cue":"","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"3:58","label":"Trembling on cue","note":"He argues that trembling can be produced at will.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"},{"t":"4:36","label":"Upset, but about what?","note":"He says it isn't all an act; the distress is real but self-directed.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"}]},{"id":"jyN5M4i26Ig","title":"Ed Sheeran’s apology video","who":"Ed Sheeran, in a video statement about a political message at one of his concerts","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"14:39","sum":"Rouse goes through Sheeran’s statement line by line. He warns that head shakes in time with words are emphasis, not lying. He notes heavy reading of a professionally written script, a louder voice without higher pitch (so no clear stress), and illustrators that grow as he relaxes into what looks like real emotion.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"0:53","label":"Head shakes are not lies","note":"Shakes in rhythm with the words emphasise them; they don't contradict them.","cue":"","ask":"nodding"},{"t":"1:58","label":"Reading a written statement","note":"Reading a script reduces natural cues.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"8:21","label":"Louder, but not higher","note":"Volume goes up while pitch stays level, so he reads no stress.","cue":"Pitch rises slightly","ask":"cue-pitch-rises-slightly"},{"t":"11:06","label":"Illustrators grow","note":"Larger head and torso illustrators where he seems to mean it.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"},{"t":"13:38","label":"Emotion once it's over","note":"Rouse reads the final emotion as genuine relief.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"Rz1qAIrjzS0","title":"Rex Heuermann’s guilty plea","who":"Rex Heuermann, who pleaded guilty to the Gilgo Beach murders (New York), at his plea hearing","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"12:32","sum":"Rouse watches Heuermann during the plea. He notes a calm he links to having given up control, and a pleasant, practised expression that fades and returns. He also points to a small head tilt and pursed lips at a point Heuermann may dispute, a slight rise in blink rate as patience wears thin, and the smile vanishing when the judge's tone hardens.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"5:15","label":"Calm after giving up control","note":"Rouse links the calm to having nothing left to decide.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"6:03","label":"A practised pleasant look","note":"One expression held throughout, which becomes his baseline.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"8:11","label":"Head tilt, pursed lips","note":"A small tilt and purse at a point he may not agree with.","cue":"Head tilt","ask":"cue-head-tilt"},{"t":"9:10","label":"Blink rate creeps up","note":"Read as patience wearing thin.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"10:04","label":"The smile disappears","note":"It fades when the judge's tone hardens.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"}]},{"id":"u8NhJzynnhQ","title":"Bernard Giles’ prison interview","who":"Bernard Giles, serving life sentences for murdering five women (Florida), in a prison interview with Piers Morgan","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"22:16","sum":"Rouse highlights the near-total absence of emotion as Giles describes his crimes: nothing changes in the brow, mouth or eyes. Seat adjustments and hand-rubs appear only when questions turn to his family, and a small smile appears when he seems unsure which emotion to show. Rouse reads the stillness as control, not peace.","concepts":["Emotion","Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"0:48","label":"No emotion at all","note":"No grief, concern or sadness while admitting to the killings.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"6:32","label":"Brow, mouth, eyes: nothing","note":"He checks each area of the face and finds no change.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"13:46","label":"Stillness as control","note":"Calm here is control, not emotional peace.","cue":"Freeze","ask":"cue-freeze"},{"t":"20:02","label":"Hands down the legs","note":"Discomfort shows when the topic turns to his family.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"},{"t":"20:14","label":"A misplaced smile","note":"A small smile where another emotion would be expected.","cue":"","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"}]},{"id":"97arHL_QXOk","title":"Dr Gerhardt Konig at his verdict","who":"Gerhardt Konig, convicted in April 2026 of attempted manslaughter of his wife (Hawaii), as the verdict was read","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"8:44","sum":"Rouse goes through Konig’s reaction to the verdict. He notes a hand rubbed on the leg, near-freezing and a high blink rate beforehand, then zero blinks while the judge speaks. Long eye-blocking follows, read as embarrassment, along with mouth-pressing and nose-touching to self-soothe. He shows stress arriving in waves, and stresses that this is normal under such pressure.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"0:21","label":"Hand rubs the leg","note":"An adaptor that releases built-up stress.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"},{"t":"0:36","label":"Almost frozen","note":"Stillness from fear and panic before the verdict.","cue":"Freeze","ask":"cue-freeze"},{"t":"3:13","label":"Blinking stops","note":"The eyes stay wide open to take everything in.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"5:10","label":"Long eye-blocking","note":"Covering the eyes and face, read as embarrassment.","cue":"","ask":"face-touching"},{"t":"5:42","label":"Pressing the mouth","note":"Self-soothing pressure on the lips.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"}]},{"id":"BuZSpKZH7R4","title":"Justin Timberlake’s DWI stop on bodycam","who":"Justin Timberlake, on police bodycam during his 2024 arrest in New York (he later pleaded guilty to a lesser driving-while-impaired charge)","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"12:46","sum":"Rouse reads the bodycam footage of the stop. He notes raised eyebrows showing engagement, illustrators out of sync with the words, long pauses, and a low voice. Defensive hands come with lowered shoulders and hands on the thighs, which he reads as submissive, not aggressive. Later, an unblinking stare as Timberlake listens hard gives way to a slow turn away as he accepts what's happening.","concepts":["Stress cues","Baseline"],"moments":[{"t":"1:13","label":"Eyebrows up: listening","note":"Raised brows show he's engaged and respectful.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"cue-eyebrow-flash"},{"t":"3:19","label":"Illustrators out of sync","note":"Hand movements that don't land on the stressed words.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"4:40","label":"Low voice, shoulders in","note":"Defensive hands but a calm, submissive posture.","cue":"Turtling (shoulders up, head down)","ask":"cue-turtling-shoulders-up-head-down"},{"t":"9:19","label":"Listening hard","note":"Very still, eyes wide, blink rate low.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"9:47","label":"Acceptance","note":"His head turns away and his shoulders drop.","cue":"Shoulders drop","ask":"cue-shoulders-drop"}]},{"id":"Y8t9N54fKKY","title":"Kouri Richins at her verdict","who":"Kouri Richins, convicted in March 2026 of murdering her husband Eric (Utah), as the verdict was read","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"9:09","sum":"Rouse follows Richins through the verdict. Before it, he sees adaptors such as hand-rubbing, lip and nose squishes, compressed lips and deep breaths. When the verdict is read, her eyes widen and her blink rate falls almost to zero, then her head drops and shoulders fall in defeat. He contrasts her reaction with the calmer sadness of the people around her.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"0:31","label":"Hands rubbed on the legs","note":"An adaptor for built-up tension.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"},{"t":"0:51","label":"Compressed lips","note":"A strong stress sign while waiting.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"2:16","label":"Eyes widen and lock","note":"She stares at the judge and scans the jury.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"2:58","label":"Defeat posture","note":"Her head drops and her shoulders fall.","cue":"Shoulders drop","ask":"cue-shoulders-drop"},{"t":"5:01","label":"Compare the room","note":"Others' sadness looks different from hers.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"Mhk0x0oA93Q","title":"Allison Mack’s interview","who":"Allison Mack, who pleaded guilty in 2019 to racketeering charges linked to NXIVM, in an interview with Ron Rosenbaum (podcast)","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"16:36","sum":"Rouse reads Mack's interview about NXIVM. He notes disgust when she talks about the 12 years she was involved, and illustrators pulled in tight as she 'gets small'. Her blink and breathing rates rise on careful answers, her voice fades at the ends of answers, a one-sided mouth raise reads as contempt, and the grief markers are missing when she expresses regret. He reads this as self-protection, and adds an interviewing tip on silence.","concepts":["Emotion","Stress cues","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"1:06","label":"Disgust at '12 years'","note":"A disgust expression as she names how long she was involved.","cue":"Disgust","ask":"cue-disgust"},{"t":"3:22","label":"Illustrators pull in","note":"She 'gets small' as gestures move close to her body.","cue":"Elbows pull in","ask":"cue-elbows-pull-in"},{"t":"5:59","label":"Blink and breath rate rise","note":"Stress while choosing words carefully.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"11:04","label":"Voice fades out","note":"Her volume drops towards the ends of answers.","cue":"","ask":"verbal-deception"},{"t":"14:23","label":"One-sided mouth raise","note":"A brief contempt display.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"}]},{"id":"73Vd-D1gKaQ","title":"Bryan Kohberger’s traffic stop","who":"Bryan Kohberger, who pleaded guilty in 2025 to the Idaho student murders, at a traffic stop weeks after the killings (2022)","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"9:19","sum":"Rouse reviews bodycam footage of a routine stop. Where most people fidget, talk a lot and blink often, Kohberger shows what Rouse calls controlled calm. His blink rate is well below normal, and his tone, posture and sentence structure all look managed. Rouse stresses that it is the whole package of small details that stands out, not any single cue.","concepts":["Baseline","Clusters","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"4:14","label":"Controlled calm","note":"Unusual stillness where most people get chatty and fidgety.","cue":"Freeze","ask":"cue-freeze"},{"t":"4:36","label":"Very low blink rate","note":"Well under the normal 15–20 a minute.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"8:35","label":"The managed package","note":"Tone, posture, word choice and blink rate together.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"}]},{"id":"8C2gVYl7Hv8","title":"Body language at the 2025 inauguration","who":"Donald Trump and Melania Trump at the January 2025 presidential inauguration (TV segment)","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"4:28","sum":"In a short TV segment, Bowden notes that sticking to the teleprompter removed Trump's usual big gestures and faces. Melania's wide hat acted as a physical barrier to an embrace, and her eyes were hidden under it. He also points to a broad smile after the oath, which he reads as real pleasure.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"0:22","label":"Script tames gestures","note":"On the teleprompter, his usual large gestures disappear.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:52","label":"The hat as a barrier","note":"A physical barrier that blocked a kiss and hid her eyes.","cue":"Arm or object as a barrier","ask":"cue-arm-or-object-as-a-barrier"},{"t":"3:16","label":"A real smile","note":"A broad smile after the vows, read as true pleasure.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"}]},{"id":"JW6sYLfO2dQ","title":"Harris vs Pence: the 2020 VP debate","who":"Kamala Harris and Mike Pence at the 2020 US vice-presidential debate","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"7:08","sum":"Bowden compares the two debaters' styles. Both use pauses and speak down the lens. Harris is steadiest with symmetrical gestures but loses breath and her voice cracks when she goes asymmetrical. Pence relies on vocal command and a stoic delivery. He frames it as a choice of style, not a verdict on either.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"1:20","label":"Strategic pauses","note":"Both pause to let points land.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"2:17","label":"Talking down the lens","note":"Eye contact with the camera speaks to viewers directly.","cue":"","ask":"eye-contact"},{"t":"2:25","label":"Symmetry steadies the voice","note":"Symmetrical gestures go with a stable voice; asymmetry throws her off.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"},{"t":"3:58","label":"Upbeat vs stoic","note":"A smile of calm pleasure against Pence's more stoic manner.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"}]},{"id":"F3EML4stgig","title":"Trump and Macron: friend or foe?","who":"Donald Trump and Emmanuel Macron during Macron's 2018 state visit to Washington","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"6:09","sum":"Bowden goes through the famous 'dandruff' moment and the long handshakes. Grooming usually signals a bond, but here the hand goes close to the neck, which reads as a status play. He points to Macron's hand-clutching and pulling, and the handshakes that turn into arm-wrestles.","concepts":["Emotion","Baseline"],"moments":[{"t":"1:27","label":"Grooming as a bond","note":"Picking at a shoulder usually signals closeness.","cue":"Grooming (hair, lint, straighten up)","ask":"cue-grooming-hair-lint-straighten-up"},{"t":"1:39","label":"Close to the neck","note":"Reaching near the neck shifts it into a dominance display.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"3:29","label":"Hand-clutching","note":"Hands held and pulled well beyond a normal handshake.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"5:06","label":"A handshake arm-wrestle","note":"Each pulls for control.","cue":"Dominant shoulder pulls back","ask":"cue-dominant-shoulder-pulls-back"}]},{"id":"XhT9f6ME33Y","title":"Trump with Trudeau at the USMCA signing","who":"Donald Trump, Justin Trudeau and Chrystia Freeland at the 2018 USMCA trade deal signing","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"6:51","sum":"Bowden reads a trade-deal signing. He notes touchy shoulder grabs, lips pressed in on tariffs (something not being said), a mouth-pull that signals doubt, and a prolonged smirk. A handshake makes Trudeau reach the extra distance, and Freeland nods along in the background. He ends with a warning that staged photos tell a planned story.","concepts":["Emotion","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"0:56","label":"Shoulder touches","note":"Reaching out to both men's shoulders.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"2:08","label":"Lips pressed in","note":"Something is held back on tariffs.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"2:37","label":"A prolonged smirk","note":"A one-sided smile held too long.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"3:27","label":"Making him reach","note":"Trudeau has to travel the extra distance for the handshake.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"6:12","label":"Stills are staged","note":"Be careful reading staged photographs.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"}]},{"id":"P5rs1v22Z3w","title":"Canada’s 2021 leaders’ debate","who":"Justin Trudeau, Erin O'Toole, Jagmeet Singh, Yves-François Blanchet and Annamie Paul at the 2021 Canadian election debate","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"6:11","sum":"Bowden compares five leaders on one stage. Trudeau holds camera eye contact with a low blink rate and looks for approval with brow raises. Singh destabilises with shrugs and asymmetry. O'Toole stays symmetrical and decided. Blanchet shows a jaw-drop of surprise, and one leader's blink rate jumps under stress.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues","Needs & approval"],"moments":[{"t":"1:07","label":"Camera lock, low blinks","note":"Steady eye contact with the camera.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"1:52","label":"Shrugs and asymmetry","note":"Read as uncertainty about siding with a rival.","cue":"Both shoulders shrug","ask":"cue-both-shoulders-shrug"},{"t":"2:26","label":"Looks for approval","note":"Eyebrow raises to check the audience agrees.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"4:27","label":"A jaw-drop","note":"Surprise displayed openly, creating a moment.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"5:21","label":"Blink rate up","note":"Higher than the others, suggesting stress.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"}]},{"id":"HuLfwTzGghs","title":"Jon Jones: fight-week body language","who":"UFC fighter Jon Jones, in press events and interviews across his career","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"13:59","sum":"Bowden follows Jones across years of fight-week footage. He contrasts a 2011 true smile with eyebrow flashes against later downward inflection and a lost liveliness. He reads hands behind the back as self-control, lip suppression under unfamiliar pressure, and body size expanding in victory and shrinking in defeat.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"1:36","label":"A true smile in 2011","note":"Light in the eyes and eyebrow flashes of recognition.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"},{"t":"5:07","label":"Optimism drains away","note":"Downward inflection and head shakes, with no smile.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"7:54","label":"Hands locked behind","note":"A way to stay self-controlled while interviewed.","cue":"Hands behind back","ask":"cue-hands-behind-back"},{"t":"8:31","label":"Lip suppression","note":"Tension and uncertainty in a new situation.","cue":"Lips pulled in / bitten","ask":"cue-lips-pulled-in-bitten"},{"t":"11:33","label":"Small in defeat, big in victory","note":"Body size tracks how a fighter feels.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"}]},{"id":"-vZNwqjdUGw","title":"Ronda Rousey before and after defeat","who":"UFC fighter Ronda Rousey, in stare-downs and interviews around her fights","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"8:59","sum":"Bowden contrasts Rousey's confident stare-downs, broken by real Duchenne smiles, with strain in her voice and missing smiles before her first loss. His lesson is to watch for changes from someone's own baseline and ask why they are happening.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"0:44","label":"A true smile","note":"Real enjoyment of the crowd.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"},{"t":"1:26","label":"Open and amicable","note":"Raised eyebrows show recognition and openness.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"cue-eyebrow-flash"},{"t":"6:48","label":"Strain in the voice","note":"Vocal strain while claiming she'll win.","cue":"Pitch rises slightly","ask":"cue-pitch-rises-slightly"},{"t":"7:19","label":"Smiles go missing","note":"Unusual for her, a change from baseline.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"8:33","label":"Look for changes","note":"Ask what changed and why.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"czOtaj-vi5U","title":"Lance Armstrong’s Oprah interview","who":"Lance Armstrong, in his 2013 interview with Oprah Winfrey in which he admitted doping","src":"Science of People (YouTube)","by":"Vanessa Van Edwards, Science of People","date":"","dur":"7:02","sum":"Van Edwards notes what is missing. Armstrong says he is sorry, but she sees no sadness (no pulled-in inner brows). What she does see is repeated contempt, a one-sided mouth raise, and a relaxed, sole-showing leg cross that she reads as confidence rather than remorse.","concepts":["Emotion","Deception cues"],"moments":[{"t":"0:42","label":"Sorry, but no sadness","note":"Remorse claimed, but no sadness on the face.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"1:03","label":"Contempt","note":"A one-sided mouth raise, seen repeatedly.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"1:57","label":"Showing the soles","note":"Read as confidence, not remorse.","cue":"","ask":""}]},{"id":"gIqg9mbeO10","title":"Presidential debates, 2000–2020","who":"US presidential candidates in televised debates from 2000 to 2020 (Bush, Gore, Kerry, Obama, McCain, Romney, Clinton, Trump, Biden)","src":"Science of People (YouTube)","by":"Vanessa Van Edwards, Science of People","date":"","dur":"29:50","sum":"Van Edwards surveys two decades of debates across both parties. Entrances and handshakes are rehearsed, but a double-touch forearm grip can build rapport. A strong answer starts with clear resonance, no breathiness and direct eye contact. Agreement cues differ from oppositional cues, and listener reactions such as eye-rolls, squints and touching the mic can undermine a rival. Her main point is that hand gestures are hard to script and often tell the truth.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"4:28","label":"The double touch","note":"A forearm touch during a handshake builds extra rapport.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"13:48","label":"How a strong answer starts","note":"Clear voice, no pauses, direct eye contact.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"14:05","label":"An anxious answer","note":"Pauses, breaths, qualifiers, throat-clearing.","cue":"Unusual pause before answering","ask":"cue-unusual-pause-before-answering"},{"t":"22:11","label":"Listener cues undermine","note":"A squint and a look away while the rival talks.","cue":"Squint","ask":"cue-squint"},{"t":"26:47","label":"Hands tell the truth","note":"Words are rehearsed; gestures are harder to script.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"}]},{"id":"0aJhLJ7K9f8","title":"Nixon vs Kennedy, 1960","who":"Richard Nixon and John F. Kennedy at the first televised US presidential debate (1960)","src":"Science of People (YouTube)","by":"Vanessa Van Edwards, Science of People","date":"","dur":"7:09","sum":"Van Edwards contrasts the two men in the first televised debate. Nixon shows 'runner's feet' (one foot pulled back), a white-knuckle grip on the chair, thigh-rubbing to self-soothe, a shifty gaze and a flash of contempt. Kennedy sits calm with planted feet and folded hands and looks straight into the camera.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"1:12","label":"Runner's feet","note":"One foot pulled back, as if ready to sprint.","cue":"Feet suddenly pulled back","ask":"cue-feet-suddenly-pulled-back"},{"t":"1:56","label":"White-knuckle grip","note":"A fist-like grip on the chair arm: anxiety.","cue":"Fingers curl (digital flexion)","ask":"cue-fingers-curl-digital-flexion"},{"t":"2:54","label":"Thigh-rubbing","note":"Self-soothing under nerves.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"},{"t":"5:21","label":"A flash of contempt","note":"A one-sided mouth raise.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"5:38","label":"Kennedy's calm","note":"A calm face and a slow, single nod.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"}]},{"id":"3ieqPfU5Uug","title":"Kamala Harris in news photos","who":"Kamala Harris, as pictured on the front pages of different news outlets","src":"Science of People (YouTube)","by":"Vanessa Van Edwards, Science of People","date":"","dur":"11:09","sum":"Van Edwards compares how different outlets' lead photos frame the same person. One image shows visible hands, a heart gesture, an exposed neck, a smile that reaches the eyes and open space between arms and torso. Another catches a surprise or fear micro-expression, no hands, and a chin tuck. Her point: a single chosen still can tilt your first impression either way.","concepts":["Emotion","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"2:25","label":"Hands first","note":"Visible hands signal intention and build trust.","cue":"Palms open","ask":"cue-palms-open"},{"t":"4:32","label":"Exposed neck, head tilt","note":"Confidence shown by exposing vulnerable areas.","cue":"Head tilt","ask":"cue-head-tilt"},{"t":"5:06","label":"A smile that reaches the eyes","note":"Genuine versus mouth-only smiles.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"},{"t":"7:25","label":"Surprise or fear","note":"Raised brows, forehead lines and the whites of the eyes.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"9:12","label":"Chin tuck","note":"An anxiety gesture that protects the neck.","cue":"Turtling (shoulders up, head down)","ask":"cue-turtling-shoulders-up-head-down"}]},{"id":"uPuEYJH-4Nk","title":"20 inaugural addresses, Truman to Trump","who":"US presidents from Truman to Trump, in their inaugural addresses","src":"Science of People (YouTube)","by":"Vanessa Van Edwards, Science of People","date":"","dur":"15:25","sum":"As a quiz, Van Edwards reports her team's coding of 20 inaugural addresses. She covers who used the most hand gestures and smiles, which party leaned on emotional language and stories versus data, and who used the most personal pronouns. Her conclusion is that the most recent presidents smiled and gestured the most.","concepts":["Pronouns & words","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"1:38","label":"Who gestures most","note":"Gesture counts compared across presidents and parties.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"6:27","label":"Emotional language by party","note":"Which side used more emotional words.","cue":"","ask":"emotion-logic"},{"t":"10:06","label":"Personal pronouns","note":"High versus low 'I' use in the addresses.","cue":"","ask":"pronoun-world"},{"t":"14:34","label":"Stories vs data","note":"Stories and intense words against data and logic.","cue":"","ask":"emotion-logic"}]},{"id":"Zvini1NZ7uw","title":"Gary Hilton’s interview","who":"Gary Hilton, convicted serial killer (Georgia and Florida murders), in a filmed interview","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"17:41","sum":"Rouse notes that Hilton's face stays blank while he describes violence. His eyes don't light up, and no expression lingers afterwards the way real ones do. His blink and breathing rates rise and his pupils seem to dilate when he talks about himself. On the 'multiple personalities' claim, his illustrators don't land where emphasis should fall.","concepts":["Emotion","Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"3:38","label":"A blank face","note":"Nothing lights up as he describes violence.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"3:53","label":"Expressions should linger","note":"Real expressions leave a trace; his leave none.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"4:33","label":"Blinks and breaths rise","note":"Heavier breathing and more blinking.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"4:48","label":"Pupils dilate","note":"As he talks about himself.","cue":"Pupils dilate","ask":"cue-pupils-dilate"},{"t":"11:28","label":"Illustrators off the beat","note":"Gestures don't land on the key words.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"}]},{"id":"X0HINHDmPh0","title":"Lori Vallow’s prison interview","who":"Lori Vallow Daybell, convicted in 2023 of murdering two of her children and in 2025 of conspiracy to murder, in a prison interview with NewsNation","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"25:38","sum":"Rouse reads Vallow Daybell's interview. Her comfort position is arms and legs crossed, and her raised chin reads as superiority. She avoids yes/no answers, and her gestures vanish whenever the murders come up. When challenged, her voice speeds up, her nostrils flare and her chin juts. When asked about remorse, her voice drops lower and slower, and she rubs her thumbs together.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"3:45","label":"Comfort position","note":"Arms and legs crossed as her base posture.","cue":"Arms crossed (relaxed fingers)","ask":"cue-arms-crossed-relaxed-fingers"},{"t":"3:54","label":"Chin up, head back","note":"Read as feeling above the interviewer.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"cue-chin-thrust"},{"t":"5:12","label":"Not a yes or no","note":"A yes/no question gets a speech.","cue":"Answer doesn't answer","ask":"cue-answer-doesn-t-answer"},{"t":"10:58","label":"Anger rises","note":"Faster voice, flared nostrils, chin forward.","cue":"Nostril flare","ask":"cue-nostril-flare"},{"t":"19:53","label":"Thumb-rubbing","note":"An adaptor as the hardest question lands.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"}]},{"id":"lHJg5L4G0II","title":"Todd Chrisley’s post-prison press conference","who":"Todd Chrisley, convicted of bank fraud and tax evasion in 2022 and pardoned in 2025, at a press conference after his release","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"24:12","sum":"Rouse builds a baseline for Chrisley at his first press conference after his release. Blinking is low under the spotlight, then rises as he relaxes. Illustrators appear on points he cares about and vanish when he's thinking. A scratch acts as an adaptor, and a one-shoulder shrug comes when he talks about why he was released.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"2:02","label":"Low blink rate","note":"Few blinks while the world is watching.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"3:53","label":"Illustrators mark emphasis","note":"His right hand emphasises key words.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"10:02","label":"Blinks rise as he relaxes","note":"The change tells you more than the level.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"12:46","label":"A small scratch","note":"An adaptor for built-up tension.","cue":"Hushing (mouth cover, cough, nose scratch)","ask":"cue-hushing-mouth-cover-cough-nose-scratch"},{"t":"14:45","label":"One-shoulder shrug","note":"On why he was released.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"}]},{"id":"tQEG26pV-VE","title":"Russell Brand’s police interview","who":"Russell Brand, convicted in November 2026 of rape and sexual assault (London), reading a prepared statement in a police interview","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"11:01","sum":"Rouse notes that Brand's normally verbose, playful baseline is gone in the interview room. An eyebrow pop greets the officer's eye contact. His illustrators stay tiny through a statement that has clearly been drafted and redrafted, his voice picks up on comfortable points, and a slow leg-rub shows stress. Rouse points out that a read statement shows very little.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"0:25","label":"Eyebrow pop","note":"A small brow raise as the officer meets his eyes.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"cue-eyebrow-flash"},{"t":"0:52","label":"Off his usual baseline","note":"Not the verbose, funny Brand people know.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"3:02","label":"Tiny illustrators","note":"Very little hand movement while denying.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"7:11","label":"Slow leg-rub","note":"Stress, which Rouse says is expected here.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"},{"t":"8:19","label":"Just reading","note":"A prepared statement shows little.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"}]},{"id":"1rEzHPheXHE","title":"Canada’s 2019 leaders’ debate","who":"Justin Trudeau, Jagmeet Singh, Maxime Bernier, Elizabeth May and Andrew Scheer at the 2019 Canadian election debate","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"8:51","sum":"Bowden coaches as much as he reads. Asymmetric gestures and looking down destabilise a speaker, and giving eye contact to someone who interrupts hands them the floor. Symmetry and hands that move with speech rhythm are easier to follow. He also covers a pen held as a pacifier to stay calm, a raised chin that can look arrogant, and a slow, low-tempo delivery.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"1:22","label":"Asymmetry destabilises","note":"Looking down with an erratic rhythm.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"},{"t":"2:37","label":"Eye contact gives the floor","note":"Looking at an interrupter lets them take over.","cue":"","ask":"eye-contact"},{"t":"3:34","label":"Symmetry and rhythm","note":"Hands moving with the speech are easier to follow.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"5:00","label":"A pen as a pacifier","note":"Something in the hand to stay calm.","cue":"Jewellery or cuff play","ask":"cue-jewellery-or-cuff-play"},{"t":"6:05","label":"Chin up reads as arrogant","note":"Over-displaying a vulnerable area.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"cue-chin-thrust"}]},{"id":"VheNm7V-F1g","title":"Biden and Putin at the 2021 Geneva summit","who":"Joe Biden and Vladimir Putin at their June 2021 summit in Geneva","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"1:43","sum":"In under two minutes, Bowden argues that Putin's 'averted eyes' were actually a turn to face the cameras. He explains how the negotiated left-hand stage position makes one arm look dominant in the handshake, and contrasts Biden's rhythmic 'baton' gestures with Putin's cooler, stoic style. He reads the staging (library, flags, globe) as part of the message.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"0:00","label":"Not averted: facing the cameras","note":"A turn to give the cameras their shot.","cue":"","ask":"eye-contact"},{"t":"0:12","label":"Stage side and dominance","note":"The left-hand position shows more of the arm.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"0:52","label":"Baton gestures","note":"Biden's rhythmic gestures, his usual baseline.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:12","label":"Cool and stoic","note":"Putin's restrained style, as expected for him.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"wOcwc0IbBCQ","title":"Prince Harry’s Brussels speech","who":"Prince Harry, giving a speech on children's online safety in Brussels","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"11:40","sum":"Rouse compares this speech with Harry's usual baseline. His arms are rigid and held close, and a concerned brow replaces his usual raised one. His cadence is slower, with falling endings and almost no fidgeting with his jacket. Line-by-line reading suggests an unfamiliar script. It livens only briefly with a facial expression and a small illustrator.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"1:02","label":"Arms held close","note":"Rigid and tight to the body: stress or shutting down.","cue":"Elbows pull in","ask":"cue-elbows-pull-in"},{"t":"1:18","label":"A concerned brow","note":"Unlike his usual raised brows.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:50","label":"Slow cadence, falling endings","note":"Voice, tone and volume all change from normal.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"3:47","label":"Reading sentence by sentence","note":"Suggests he hadn't rehearsed it.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"6:42","label":"A flicker of expression","note":"The first real expression, then back down.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"dVdRICXol4Y","title":"Conor McGregor’s fight-week bravado","who":"UFC fighter Conor McGregor, at press events and weigh-ins","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"7:31","sum":"Bowden reads McGregor's showmanship: a huge victory pose at the weigh-in and aggression that increasingly looks orchestrated. Unusual mouth activity and lip suppression might be a 'tell' of doubt to watch for in future fights. Even after a loss, he keeps the flamboyance rather than showing the body language of defeat.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"1:52","label":"A huge victory pose","note":"Status display at the weigh-in.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"3:42","label":"Unusual mouth activity","note":"Not typical for him, so worth noting.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"4:13","label":"Lip suppression","note":"Possibly the same behaviour he shows in defeat.","cue":"Lips pulled in / bitten","ask":"cue-lips-pulled-in-bitten"},{"t":"5:52","label":"No defeat display","note":"Even after a loss, he keeps the bravado.","cue":"","ask":""}]}],"concepts":["Baseline","Clusters","Deception cues","Stress cues","Pronouns & words","Needs & approval","Emotion"],"noteHughes":"Chase Hughes’ own analysis: mostly <i>The Behavior Panel</i>, where he analyses alongside Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden, plus a TV clip from his channel. Summaries are my own words. Timestamps were checked against each video’s captions.","noteOthers":"<b>Not Chase Hughes.</b> Other analysts, kept separate, for comparison only. Their methods and opinions are their own and not part of the Hughes-only Ask answers."};
